# Target C — regression: four boundary hosts annihilate perimeter-subtracted signed return

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** exact finite geometric regression for the all-regime signed-return route.  
**Dependencies:** none beyond the Section-117 signed-return definitions.

Section 117 gets a positive return constant because at most three directed host directions must serve the four signed train directions `u,-u,v,-v` of a strict rhombus. This note records the sharp obstruction to simply replacing the three principal routes by *all* outer boundary routes.

With four hosts, a large triangle-free strict-rhombus grid can have

\[
A_4=\Theta(D^2)
\]

but **zero** perimeter-subtracted route excess and **zero** signed-return loss on every train. Thus an all-regime theorem must prevent a fourth effective host from carrying a linear train population. The three-host mechanism itself cannot be extended to arbitrary outer routes.

---

## 1. Oblique square-grid lattice

Choose two unit vectors `u,v` with angle

\[
\theta\in(\pi/3,2\pi/3).
\tag{1.1}
\]

Let

\[
\Lambda=\{iu+jv:i,j\in\mathbb Z\}.
\]

For one lattice difference,

\[
|au+bv|^2=a^2+b^2+2ab\cos\theta.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Since `|cos theta|<1/2`,

\[
|au+bv|^2
\ge a^2+b^2-|ab|.
\tag{1.3}
\]

For nonzero integers `(a,b)`, the right side is at least one. If both coordinates are nonzero,

\[
a^2+b^2-|ab|
=(|a|-|b|)^2+|ab|\ge1;
\]

if one coordinate vanishes, the claim is immediate.

Equality in the actual norm occurs for the basis vectors `+-u,+-v`. Because the angle is strictly between the two triangular endpoints, `u-v` and `u+v` both have length strictly greater than one. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{the only unit lattice vectors are }\pm u,\pm v.
}
\tag{1.4}
\]

The lattice is unit-separated and its unit-contact graph is the ordinary square grid in the two basis directions. In particular it contains no unit-contact triangle.

---

## 2. Finite rhombic patch

For integers `M,N>=1`, take

\[
X_{M,N}:=\{iu+jv:0\le i\le M,\ 0\le j\le N\}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Its contact graph consists exactly of the horizontal and vertical grid edges. The bounded faces are `MN` strict unit rhombi, each of area `sin theta`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{A_4=MN\sin\theta.}
\tag{2.2}
\]

The convex hull `P` is the parallelogram with side vectors `Mu` and `Nv`. Its contact outer walk follows the four straight boundary chains, so

\[
L=2M+2N=\operatorname{per}(P),
\qquad
P_{\rm exc}=0.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Consequently the perimeter-subtracted physical route excess is exactly

\[
\boxed{L+P_{\rm exc}-\operatorname{per}(P)=0.}
\tag{2.4}
\]

while `A_4` is quadratic when `M,N` are comparable.

---

## 3. Four hosts make every signed-return loss vanish

Direct the four boundary chains in their outer-walk orientations. Their unit chord/host directions are

\[
Q=\{u,v,-u,-v\}.
\tag{3.1}
\]

For a train parallel to `u`,

\[
\max_{q\in Q}q\cdot u=1,
\qquad
\min_{q\in Q}q\cdot u=-1,
\]

so

\[
w_Q(u)=2.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Likewise

\[
w_Q(v)=2.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Every complete train in the patch has direction `u` or `v`. Hence every Section-117 train return term is exactly zero:

\[
\boxed{2-w_Q(u_\tau)=0\quad\text{for every complete train }\tau.}
\tag{3.4}
\]

The signed-return inequality is sharp here: both sides of the perimeter-subtracted version are zero even though every rhombus is strict and the rhombic area is quadratic.

---

## 4. Why the Section-117 three-host constant is sharp in host number

Section 117 proves that for two strict-rhombus directions and at most three unit hosts,

\[
(2-w_Q(u))+(2-w_Q(v))\ge2-\sqrt3>0.
\tag{4.1}
\]

The present example shows that replacing `|Q|<=3` by `|Q|<=4` destroys every positive universal constant:

\[
\boxed{|Q|=4\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
(2-w_Q(u))+(2-w_Q(v))=0}
\tag{4.2}
\]

for the natural four hosts of the oblique parallelogram.

Thus the host-count step is not an artifact of the proof. It is the exact combinatorial source of the signed-return gap.

---

## 5. Consequence for the live all-regime route

A tempting response to the Section-116 isoperimetric-dominance regression is to assign every outer train endpoint to its actual consecutive-attachment route and compare signed return directly with

\[
L+P_{\rm exc}-\operatorname{per}(P).
\]

The present finite regression shows that no theorem of the form

\[
d\bigl(L+P_{\rm exc}-\operatorname{per}(P)\bigr)
\ge cA_4
\]

with an absolute `c>0` can hold for arbitrary strict-rhombic carriers using unrestricted outer hosts.

A successful all-regime theorem must instead prove a **source-specific effective-host restriction**. In the language of Sections 117--118, it must show that a fourth (and subsequent) route family cannot carry enough distinct physical train-end occurrences to absorb the quadratic rhombic area. Equivalently, one needs a theorem of the form

```text
large fourth-host endpoint population
    => interface / route / diameter-minimality contradiction.
```

This pinpoints the structural content hidden by the small-`sigma` estimates: they work because all but three principal host corridors have small endpoint capacity. Extending them to all regimes requires controlling fourth-host capacity, not changing the return constant.

---

## 6. Scope

- The patches `X_{M,N}` are local regressions, not claimed global diameter minimizers.
- No source point is deleted and the lattice is exactly unit-separated.
- Triangle-freeness is exact because the only unit vectors are `+-u,+-v`.
- The obstruction persists for arbitrarily large finite patches and for angles arbitrarily close to `pi/3` from above.
- The regression does not invalidate Sections 117--118; it identifies the exact hypothesis that makes their positive return possible.

**Disposition:** exact regression. An all-regime signed-return closure must control effective fourth-host capacity.